    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAAMX//+tE\u003d",
        "filename": "chip/stm32/keyboard_scan.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 176,
      "author": {
        "id": 1001923
      },
      "writtenOn": "2012-07-26T00:35:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m pretty sure this is necessary.  If we only check in keyboard_scan_task, then any time the power button interrupt fires while the ec is in check_keys_changed or select_column, there\u0027s a good chance that it\u0027ll set the problematic column low before it notices the flag has been set.  My thinking was that putting it here is as close to the actual call that sets their tri-state value as is possible to reduce the risk.",
      "parentUuid": "AAAAMX//+5k\u003d",
      "revId": "eb8679ec38fec90f6ebaef3bf0f6814700b62383",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAAMX//+s4\u003d",
        "filename": "chip/stm32/keyboard_scan.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 176,
      "author": {
        "id": 1001923
      },
      "writtenOn": "2012-07-26T00:35:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If col !\u003d COL_TRI_STATE_ALL, it should be exactly the same as before.  If the scan is 1/2 way through the problematic column when the button is pressed, it will still stil trying to read from it, and the power-button interrupt handler should push everything into hi-z right when it\u0027s pressed(in keyboard_enable_scanning), so it should be okay I think",
      "parentUuid": "AAAAMX//++E\u003d",
      "revId": "eb8679ec38fec90f6ebaef3bf0f6814700b62383",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
